Subject: Does RH6.0 installation really suck?

Or what did I do wrong?

I first have some ranting, then some questions and finally a proposal.

<rant>
I finally got the network install going (thanks to one piece of information
that the ethernet driver for the Multia doesn't work for the thinnet part, so
I found an old transceiver to connect to the AUI port), but after many attempts
to install a reduced RedHat 6.0 to a 200MB disk (16MB swap and the rest for / )
I have now given up and turn to you, the knowledgeable bunch.

The install never gives a credible number for how much space the selection of
packages will take up, regardless of whether I try just to install from the
general selection menu or from the select individual packages menu. I mean, I
get different numbers for each package, the total install, how much I am
'missing' and if I want to continue anyway.

Also, even if I deselect certain packages they are installed anyway, and if I
only have ca.184MB installing the kernel tree and perl 'by mistake' isn't
really acceptable because I run out of space.

Finally, I sat dumbfounded because the installation routine seems to install
packages by alphabetic order, EVEN though it knows that I will get into trouble
because I have selected to 'install anyway' (I have less space than it seems to
think I will need) and crucial packages gets installed after mere application
packages just because they are after in the alphabet?!?

I just sit staring on the screen while I see that the kernel tree is installed
even though I didn't select it (and gcc, g++ with libraries, perl and what have
we) and start wishing that Slackware would come out with an Alpha distribution
because there we at least have some coherency during the install.
</rant>

<questions>
1. What did I do wrong?
2. How am I able to install a reduced set of packages with RH6.0?
3. Or *is* RH only able to install 'everything' with this?
4. If ( 3.==True ) how is RH6.0 different from Win98?
5. Where is the 'easy part' of this installation compared to eg. Slackware,
   which is the only one (apart from SLS) I know about previously.
</questions>

<proposal>
I have seen a precious posting on this list about no emergency boot/root floppy
exists for any Alpha distribution. I believe that if installing everything is
the only thing people can think of, but if anyone is interested in something
more effective and flexible you can't possibly find it satisfying (IMNSHO).

I also noticed that my floppy drive is a 2.88MB drive so there is lots of
room for a variety of packages, if this is in fact standard on the Alpahs. If
anyone in the know could let me know about this, please do. If it isn't, we
would have to use 1.44MB as the standard size. Would anyone be interested in
producing an emergency set of floppies? I have had good use for that with all
the PCs (i386) I have because when you fiddle with it you will sometimes screw
up and need to get back from down-under :-)

I don't know how to make the final image that eventually needs to be dd'ed to
the floppy, so if anyone knows about that please let me know.

After that I would like to figure out how to make a Slackware like distribution
for the Alpha (I haven't asked Voelkerding yet) because that is the ultimate in
ease and flexibility of installation so if anyone has any idea I wouldn't mind
hearing. Otherwise I'll just do it myself. Sound config sortof lacks in
Slackware, I know that, but that is periferal to me on getting a system itself
running, so that was never my main concern.

Especially with all these previous NT-dudes we will need something, and with
guys like me that hadn't used anything but i386 before (with Linux) and now got
a Multia because eBiz sold them 'real cheap' and as they had 1300 of them there
has got to be a lot of Alpha Newbies with a total different background than you
old rags that just know what its all about.
</proposal>

So if you have something you want to say about this, please come forward. If
you don't like what I write please feel free to flame, because a nugget of info
may be buried even there and I don't mind :-)

Peter
