On Tue, 7 Sep 1999, yue-feng sun wrote:
<snip>
>Installation was finally "successful" by getting " Congratulations. The
>installation is complete". However, I could not boot from /dev/sdb1 by 
>using (at the MILO> prompt)
>	boot sdb1:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sdb1
>After rebooting using Linux/RH4.2, I found that there is no
>vmlinux.gz under root / on /dev/sdb1. Instead, I found vmlinuz under /boot. 
>So I used 
>	boot sdb1:boot/vmlinuz root=/dev/sdb1
>on the second time to reboot RH6.0 and then it was ok. And then
>I copied the MILO (linload.exe) to /dev/sda1 using 
>dd if=/dev/fd0 of=/dev/sda1 bs=1440k
>		
>	The question is why there is no vmlinux.gz under root as usually it does
>even for "successful and complete installtion"?

RedHat synced this behaviour with their Intel Version. The idea is, that
you can put /boot on a separat partition somewhere near the beginning of
the disk so you don't have to worry about Intel Bios limitations (etc. 
8 GB barrier) during boot time and at the same time allow other OSes to
reside completly at the beginning.

          Helge

