Greg Lindahl wrote:
> 
> > I certainly hope Compaq are bright enough to figure out
> > that by trying to force high price peripherals on Alpha
> > users, they are only hurting the sales of their boxes.
> 
> You are making a huge assumption.
> 

See comments below.

> First, SRM now supports IDE, so there's no question that you can boot
> cheaply.

True. But, have you tried to buy an IDE equipped system
from Compaq, say in the past 2 months?? Quite hard to get
them to agree to sell them. One of the requirements is
that you MUST buy a disk of some sort from them, at least
with DS10's. On the other hand, it could be the reseller
with the restriction but they say it's Compaq's problem.
They would sell it any way I want it if they could.

> 
> Second, while Linux does support a variety of SCSI cards, it's often
> with much pain, loathing, and expense.

It seems clear the Symbios/NCR driver has much support from the 
company that makes those chips. I seriously doubt they would 
deny Compaq that same resource. The Intraserver post gives a
clue to the real problem: "If it had been an 895 or 896 based 
board, it would not have run at all if it wasn't licensed.... "

> If I were you, 

Keep in mind you're not me. I have to make $$$$ with my systems.
What you do at your university has little bearing on what I do
with processors in my business.

I don't particularly care what chip I use as long as it gives
me cheap MFLOPS. I've got problems that only get bigger and I
already run problems that last 20+ days on 32 RS/6000 power3 
processors, read 400+GB of input data multiple times, and create 
16GB images.

> I wouldn't be
> that surprised that the SRM guys have trouble supporting the same
> cards. I certainly wouldn't start insulting Compaq's intelligence or
> engineering competance as a result. Sheesh!
> 

I wouldn't call it an insult. I'd call it a comparison. I suspect
the latter part of my previous statement is the true reason. I
don't care what TRU64 supports but I do care what SRM can support
and it does need to support standard Intel-centric cards if they
expect to compete in the same arena. The price/performance of Intels
are respectable, and in fact are quite competitive with the latest
Alphas if you can tolerate 3-4 times as many boxes to do the work,
2 times if you go dual PIII's. It's strictly a space issue from
my view point that I buy an Alpha vs multiple PIII's. I expect if
dual Athlons can outperform dual PIII's by a significant amount, that
going with dual Athlon based systems will be quite attractive
compared to Alphas and I hope Compaq keep that in mind.


Wes


