Praveen,
	Whenever you possibly can, you want to use aboot/SRM.  When booting 
Linux using the SRM, Linux uses the Tru64 Palcode.

Significant amounts of Compaq resources are devoted to guarenteeing that any
bugs in the SRM/Tru64 Palcode are fixed.  By using SRM, Linux can go along for
the ride. 

There are known bugs in the freeware (MILO) Palcode, (such as non-working 
perfomance counter support), that could be a real problem when trying to 
run high performance code. 

I think that any use of MILO will cause the freeware palcode to be used, so
just stick to aboot/SRM if you can. (Jay, Am I right here?) 

--Phil

