At the risk of sounding like an idiot after having read some FAQs 
related to Linux and Alphas...

Question 1:
I know nothing about Alpha machines, but picked one up for a tiny sum
from an acquaintance to play with. I would like some help identifying
it. The stuff on the outside didn't help me identify it against anything
in the FAQ. 

Small "pizza-box" style with stand to sit it up on end, "DIGITAL"
clearly emblazoned by the round power button. Back has usual PS/2 mouse
& keyboard, serial, parallel, with additional built-in video, ethernet
(10-base T/2/AUI) connectors. The sucker seems to run pretty darned hot.
(The CPU has a monsterous heat sink with single fan next to it -- above
when in the stand.) I've five other machines in this room, which has its
own small air conditioner to keep the room from catching fire. *grin* 

Upon power-up it says:

Multia SRM Console BL5 V3.7-620, built on Apr 25 1995 at 15:14:12
*** Warning: NVR/TOY battery is dead ***
Testing memory from 8 to 32 meg...
Wrong QSNVR version - Expected V1.0, Received: V0.0
Initializing QSNVR...
Loading ARC firmware...

at which point it hangs. 

Question 2a/b:
Could somebody briefly interpret the power-up messages? Is it supposed
to hang? I presume that it's due to the battery being dead? This 
machine's been sitting on the shelf for a couple of years.

Question 3:
It has no hard drive and does not appear to have room inside for one. I
presume that this machine was designed to be a diskless client? I
presume I can set this machine up to boot diskless? 

The fellow from whom I bought the machine gave me an old AT baby tower
with mini-50 SCSI connector and SCSI CD-ROM. He said that he had the
computer running Linux on a HD in the external "case." Assuming this
machine works well I want to add a HD and use it for a small web server.
I think that I have a older, small SCSI HD laying around somewhere.

THANK YOU!

James


