Hi, 

When your machine boots, look closely at the command
line prompt you get for a moment just before x starts.
Above the prompt the version of the kernel is printed.
If the word "BOOT" is part of the kernal name, then go
to your /boot directory and look at the kernels
available there. One will be the *BOOT* kernal. That
is the kernel that was used during the install. It
doesn't have vfat support in it. Change the kernal you
boot to.
Hope this helps.

David


