Hi Jaswinder!
As it is tuesday already I  believe you managed to get minlabel going.
If this it not the case I would be happy to assist you.
Some thoughts on partitioning...
Aboot requires some space of its own at the physical beginning of the
disk (something greater 1.44 MB should do the trick. You will have to
put the initial kernel image there as well (copyboot)). I always created
a partition for aboot (starting at offset zero) but someone explained
that it is not needed, if your first partition starts at an offset that
leaves ~2mb for the booting machinery.
At least older aboots have problems reaching a bootable kernel if it
resides on a partition that crosses the 2 gb barrier. Therefore it
seemed wise to me to reserve a special partition for boot kernels (say
some dozens mb as partition b:). This partition could be mounted under
/boot (I believe that this is the new rh behaviour anyway).
Have swap and all the other partitions as you like.

Some thoughts on minlabel...
Minlabel requires that you give size and offset of the partitions as
well as the fstype (use ext2 for system, swap for swap). The size is
given in 512 byte "blocks". The offset is calculated as the sum of all 
previously defined sizes. (If you use a first partition for aboot that
gives offset zero). In bsd-disklabling it seems to be customary to
define c: as the whole disk from offset zero to the last block. This is
NOT required in alphalinux. And beware some people have pointed out that
going to the last cylinder might lead to data corruption by turnover (I
do not recall whether this affects  "fdisk -b" and minlabel as well).
Stars in the cylinder  column indicate that partitions do not end at
cylinder boundaries (simply recalculate right sizes from the given
information about the sector mapping).
This is my collected minlabel wisdom, I hope that helps (if help should
still be needed).

Good luck
	Sebastian Möller
