Hi folks,

On an AlphaPC164LX...

A while ago, I compiled glibc-2.1.2pre3 using a slightly patched gcc 2.95.1
(patch from Stephen L Moshier found in the c9x-complex package).  I had
basically no problems with this one.

Today, I tried compiling the latest glibc-2.1.2 (final, I guess), and there
were many more math problems during the testing.  I did a quick diff, and found
very little change in the math code... Strange.  Any thoughts what migth be
causing the new failures ?  I upgraded my kernel from 2.2.3 to 2.2.13pre11
between the two compilations.  Can this be the cause ?

The other strange thing is when trying to link something with -lpthread I get:
/usr/lib/libpthread.so: undefined reference to `__libc_fork@@GLIBC_2.1.2'

Any thoughts ?

The last strange thing I have is that lnxcc, the Compaq C compiler, refuses to 
run (both with glibc-2.1.2pre3 and 2.1.2 final).  It runs for a while, then 
stops with the message:
/usr/lib/compaq/ccc-6.2/alpha-redhat-linux/bin/lnxcc:
 error in loading shared libraries:
 /usr/lib/compaq/ccc-6.2/alpha-redhat-linux/bin/lnxcc:
 undefined symbol: __setjmp

nm finds the __setjmp in the libc.so file.

The end of an strace looks like this:
... Reads the C source file, then
close(4)                                = 0
munmap(0x20000030000, 8192)             = 0
rt_sigaction(SIGTRAP, {0x120399840, [], 0}, NULL, 8, 0) = 0
rt_sigaction(SIGFPE, {0x120399840, [], 0}, NULL, 8, 0) = 0
rt_sigaction(SIGILL, {0x120399840, [], 0}, NULL, 8, 0) = 0
osf_setsysinfo(0xe, 0x11fffa960, 0, 0x1, 0x120310760) = 0
write(2, "/usr/lib/compaq/ccc-6.2/alpha-re"..., 52) = 52
write(2, ": error in loading shared librar"..., 37) = 37
write(2, "/usr/lib/compaq/ccc-6.2/alpha-re"..., 52) = 52
write(2, ": ", 2)                       = 2
write(2, "undefined symbol: __setjmp", 26) = 26
write(2, "", 0)                         = 0
write(2, "", 0)                         = 0
write(2, "\n", 1)                       = 1
exit(127)                               = ?

Any hints greatly appreciated.

Cheers,
					Christian



