This sounds like a useful file somewhere has got corrupted - probably
you've lost the XF86Config file or something.  Can you telnet into it
remotely ?

If you can then edit /etc/inittab - you should find a line which says:

id:5:initdefault:

The 5 is normally the one to startup in X, change that to 3  and reboot -
that should let you boot to a plain text mode so you can sort it out.

Then login as root and run the 'xf86config' program to reconfigure X.

