Dan,

At 07:46 PM 9/24/99 +1000, you wrote:
>On Fri, 24 Sep 1999, Quant-X Alpha Linux Support wrote:
>
>> 
>> Hi Daniel !
>> 
>> Interesting. I had a SB/128 with Ensoniq-Chip running under 
>> Linux/AXP (together with a Hauppage Win/TV) (LX board, OSS).
>> 
>> I understand that it can't work on Jensen. ;-}
>> But why should it not work on SX ?
>
>I never said it wouldn't work on an SX :) Just that it doesn't work on my
>UX. Infact I know of someone that couldn't get it to work on an LX, and
>someone else (other than you) who could. Again, I think it is how the
>IRQ's get mapped. For example (and I don't know if this is technically
>correct, but it was something to do with a lack of bits..) if the PCI
>chipset tells the card it's irq is something greater than 2^4 then any
>other bits get masked out. So, when on my UX it gets mapped to IRQ 44, I
>never receive the interrupt correctly. Of course, this is not entirely
>correct - internally it is mapped to something altogether different. I
>dont know much about how the PCI chipsets handle this sort of thing or
>even if this is the exact problem, but it would explain why it works on
>all Intels (having 16 mapped interrupts) and not always on an Alpha. And
>you cant trust the value that /proc/pci returns, internally they can be
>different.

The problem with the UX is the PCI bridge which is used on that system to
extend PCI slot number, not performance.  The primary PCI is a 64 bit slot
(5 volt keyed, 33 MHz) while the remaining 4 PCI slots are 32 bit (5 volt,
33 MHz) and are bridged off the primary PCI bus rather than connecting the
Northbridge CIA/P controller directly (as in a peer PCI arrangement, i.e.
multiple PCI buses).  Many devices do not comply with the PCI bridge
specification 1.0, this is the problem with most PCI sound cards and more
recently modems.  The SX and LX (which the core chipset, Pyxis 21174) is
the same, the PCI bus arrangement is different and does not bridge the 32
bit PCI slots to the 64 bit PCI slots (they are one contiguous, non
segmented bus). The UX used the technique to address PCI bus load issues
and extend the number of slots cheaply.

The of most PCI implementations being more or less bound to the brain-dead
16 IRQ limit of the x86 is a byproduct of Intel's participation in the spec
and its implementation by manufacturers largely targetting that same brain
dead commodity market.

